
  [1mClassification: bounded[0m — the login flow already exists here, in one file, with one caller. So I'll present a short
  design in chat rather than write a spec. Say the word if you want it treated as bigger.

  One thing blocks me from just doing it: [1mnothing in this app currently has a userId.[0m The form has username and
  password; the submit handler has no other source of identity. So "add a userId parameter" needs a source, and the
  three plausible sources lead to different code and different meanings of "who logged in":

  - [1mGenerated client-side per attempt[0m (e.g. [38;5;153mcrypto.randomUUID()[39m in the submit handler) — buys you a correlation ID that
    ties this attempt's logs together. Costs nothing and adds no new input. But it identifies the [3mattempt[0m, not the
    person; it can't answer "which account logged in" on its own. Cheap to change later.
  - [1mSame value as username[0m — matches what the form actually knows about identity. But then the parameter is redundant
    with [38;5;153musername[39m, and you get a signature carrying the same value twice. Cheap to change, but arguably not worth
    adding.
  - [1mA real account ID supplied by the caller[0m — the only option that genuinely tracks [3mwho[0m, and the one that matches how
    this would work against [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m. Costs the most: nothing in the client has an account ID before login succeeds,
    so it has to come from somewhere new (a new form field, a stored value, or the login response). This is the one
    that's expensive to undo, because it changes where identity comes from.

  My recommendation is the [1mclient-side correlation ID[0m, because this is a stub that only [38;5;153mconsole.log[39ms — a per-attempt ID
  makes the logging actually useful today without inventing an identity source the app doesn't have. If what you really
  want is account-level tracking, that's the third option and it's a larger change than adding a parameter.
